; basics.loon — Guide: Basics
[fn guide-basics-page []
  [doc-page
    "Basics"
    "Expressions, bindings, and fundamental syntax."
    
    [doc-section
      "Everything is an Expression"
      [doc-text
        "Hold onto one idea and the rest of Loon falls into place: "
        "there are no statements. Every construct produces a value. "
        [doc-code "if"]
        " returns a value. "
        [doc-code "let"]
        " returns a value. "
        "A block of expressions returns its last one. Once this clicks, your code changes shape. "
        "You stop arranging steps in the order \"do this, then do that\" and start "
        "composing answers in the form \"this evaluates to that.\""]
      [code-block
        {}
        [str
          "[let x [if true 1 2]]  ; x = 1\n"
          "[let y [do\n"
          "  [println \"side effect\"]\n"
          "  42]]                  ; y = 42"]]
      [doc-text
        "In the second example, "
        [doc-code "do"]
        " runs both expressions in sequence, "
        "yet the whole block evaluates to "
        [doc-code "42"]
        ", because that is the last expression. "
        "Side effects happen along the way; the value flows through."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Bracket Syntax"
      [doc-text
        "Every function call wears the same uniform: square brackets, the function first, "
        "then its arguments. There is no special syntax "
        "for operators, method calls, or macros. One shape covers everything: "
        [doc-code "[function arg1 arg2]"]
        ". The uniformity is the point. "
        "You trade a brief unfamiliarity for a language with no precedence rules to memorize "
        "and no question about where the parentheses go."]
      [code-block
        {}
        [str
          "[println \"hello\"]      ; call println\n"
          "[+ 1 2]                ; call +\n"
          "[map inc #[1 2 3]]     ; call map"]]
      [doc-text
        "Even "
        [doc-code "+"]
        " is a function, with no special standing among the rest."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Primitives"
      [doc-text
        "The primitive types are deliberately few: strings, integers, floats, and booleans. "
        "Nothing converts between them on its own, so "
        [doc-code "1 + 1.0"]
        " never coerces behind your back. When you want a string, you reach for "
        [doc-code "str"]
        "; for numbers, you call the conversion you mean."]
      [code-block
        {}
        [str
          "[let name \"Loon\"]      ; Str\n"
          "[let age 1]            ; Int\n"
          "[let pi 3.14159]       ; Float\n"
          "[let ok true]          ; Bool"]]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Let Bindings"
      [doc-text
        "Use "
        [doc-code "let"]
        " to bind a value to a name. Bindings are immutable "
        "by default: once you have bound "
        [doc-code "x"]
        " to "
        [doc-code "10"]
        ", that is what it stays. Coming from a mutable-by-default "
        "language, this can feel like a cage. It is closer to a guarantee, retiring every bug "
        "that begins with the question \"who changed this variable?\""]
      [code-block
        {}
        [str "[let x 10]\n" "[let y [+ x 5]]\n" "[println y]  ; 15"]]
      [doc-note
        "info"
        "There is no def keyword in Loon. Use let for all bindings."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Comments"
      [doc-text
        "Line comments start with a semicolon. Put one on its own line "
        "or at the end of an expression; everything after the semicolon is yours to say."]
      [code-block
        {}
        [str "; This is a comment\n" "[let x 42]  ; inline comment"]]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Vectors, Maps, and Sets"
      [doc-text
        "Three collection literals cover most of what you reach for. Vectors use "
        [doc-code "#[...]"]
        " for ordered sequences. Maps use "
        [doc-code "{:key value}"]
        " for key-value associations, with keywords (the "
        [doc-code ":name"]
        " syntax) "
        "as the usual key. Sets use "
        [doc-code "#{...}"]
        " for unordered, unique collections."]
      [code-block
        {}
        [str
          "[let nums #[1 2 3]]\n"
          "[let user {:name \"Ada\" :age 30}]\n"
          "[let tags #{\"fast\" \"safe\"}]"]]
      [doc-text
        "All three are immutable. Operations like "
        [doc-code "append"]
        " or "
        [doc-code "assoc"]
        " hand back a new collection and leave the original alone. "
        "The Collections guide covers these in detail."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "If and Do"
      [doc-text
        "Because "
        [doc-code "if"]
        " is an expression, it always produces a value. "
        "It takes a condition, a then-branch, and an optional else-branch, and each branch is a "
        "single expression. When a branch needs to do several things, wrap them in "
        [doc-code "[do ...]"]
        "."]
      [code-block
        {}
        [str
          "[if [> x 0]\n"
          "  \"positive\"\n"
          "  \"non-positive\"]\n"
          "\n"
          "[if ready\n"
          "  [do\n"
          "    [println \"starting\"]\n"
          "    [run]]]"]]
      [doc-text
        "Why no implicit "
        [doc-code "do"]
        " inside "
        [doc-code "if"]
        "? Because a branch usually needs one expression, and the "
        "explicit "
        [doc-code "do"]
        " marks the moment you reach for side effects. "
        "A small ceremony that keeps the code honest about what it does."]
      [doc-note
        "warning"
        "if branches do NOT have implicit do. Use [do ...] to run multiple expressions in a branch."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Printing"
      [doc-text
        "Use "
        [doc-code "println"]
        " to print a value with a newline, "
        "or "
        [doc-code "print"]
        " without one. To assemble a string from "
        "several pieces, "
        [doc-code "str"]
        " concatenates its arguments into one."]
      [code-block
        {}
        [str "[println \"hello world\"]\n" "[println [str \"x = \" x]]"]]]
    
    [doc-prev-next "By Example" "/examples" "Functions" "/guide/functions"]]]
